WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.316
Here are eight independent jobs.

2
00:00:02.316 --> 00:00:05.068
Each block keeps the same duration
throughout.

3
00:00:05.068 --> 00:00:07.820
One worker must finish them in sequence.

4
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.966
These are illustrative work units, not
measured seconds.

5
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:15.370
With four workers, several jobs run at
once.

6
00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:19.146
When a worker finishes, it takes the next
waiting job.

7
00:00:19.146 --> 00:00:21.033
No individual job becomes faster.

8
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:27.170
Watch the same clock on both schedules.

9
00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:31.198
The parallel run finishes in seven units
instead of twenty-four.

10
00:00:31.198 --> 00:00:35.226
Overlapping independent work shortens the
time until everything is done.

11
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:40.038
Near the end, some workers are idle while
others finish.

12
00:00:40.038 --> 00:00:42.251
This uneven tail prevents perfect scaling.

13
00:00:42.251 --> 00:00:45.940
More workers cannot split a job that must
stay together.

14
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.258
Now include setup and the final join.

15
00:00:51.258 --> 00:00:52.920
Those serial steps remain.

16
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.829
Total time becomes twenty-seven units
versus ten.

17
00:00:55.829 --> 00:00:59.568
Parallel work helps, but serial work
limits the gain.
